se¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ8for all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙSbeing justified freely by his grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus:¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book,§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ®whom God hath set forth to be a propitiation through faith in his blood, to declare his righteousness for the remission of sins that are past, through the forbearance of God;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ|to declare, I say, at this time his righteousness: that he might be just, and the justifier of him which believeth in Jesus.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book,§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ\Where is boasting then? It is excluded. By what law? of works? Nay: but by the law of faith.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙTTherefore we conclude that a man is justified by faith without the deeds of the law.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book,§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙZIs he the God of the Jews only? is he not also of the Gentiles? Yes, of the Gentiles also:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfseeing it is one God, which shall justify the circumcision by faith, and uncircumcision through faith.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRDo we then make void the law through faith? God forbid: yea, we establish the law.¦layout ªlast_verse